{"agent":"bugs","findings":[{"id":"bugs-001","file":"src/angular/src/app/pages/main/notification-bell.component.html","line":28,"title":"Notification text is now parsed as HTML, so server and remote error strings can inject markup and lose text","category":"logic-error","cwe":"CWE-79","severity":"high","agent_confidence":82,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The change from `{{ notif.text }}` to `[innerHTML]=\"notif.text\"` makes the browser parse every notification string as HTML. Some of those strings come from outside the app: `status.server.errorMessage` (header.component.ts:49), `status.controller.latestRemoteScanError` passed through STATUS_REMOTE_SERVER_ERROR (header.component.ts:96, localization.ts:15-16), `reaction.errorMessage` (settings-page.component.ts:170/239/257) and `result.errorMessage` passed through Bulk.ERROR (file-list.component.ts:554). None of them is escaped. No Localization template contains markup (localization.ts:15-19, 74-77), so nothing needed HTML rendering. The only effect is that these strings are now read as markup. Angular's DomSanitizer removes scripts and event handlers, but it still allows tags such as `&lt;a href&gt;`, `&lt;img src=https://...&gt;` and styling elements. A remote-server or SSH error string can therefore insert a working link or a tracking image into the app UI. That is content injection, not script execution. Any text that looks like a tag is also deleted: a path such as `/data/&lt;show name&gt;` or an SSH stderr line like `&lt;user&gt;@host` loses that part. Characters like `&amp;lt;` are decoded, so the notification shows different text than was sent. This also breaks the `.text !==` change detection in header.component.ts:54/98, which compares the raw strings while the UI shows a sanitized version. Nearby code works the other way: confirm-modal.service.ts:33 escapes HTML before inserting it. The user's CLAUDE.md also forbids rendering user-supplied content through innerHTML.","current_code":"&lt